An alternative was to keep "fetch once before starting" but demand
user confirmation first. That still bakes in the assumption that
fresher refs are what the run wants; the retrospective's point was the
opposite - the refs as they stand are the target, and the user may
have pinned them deliberately. So the confirmation moved to the only
case where fetching is even on the table: visibly stale refs, before
anything starts.

"If the refs look stale" is left to judgment. No staleness probe is
prescribed - FETCH_HEAD's mtime was considered and dropped, since it
only says when some fetch last ran, not whether origin has moved. If
this proves too vague in practice, a follow-up could compare against
the remote-tracking ref's reflog.
